L75: # mmc1
L76: Online Supplement
L77: Methods:
L78: Protocol Synopsis:
L79: This was designed as a double-blinded, placebo-controlled crossover study of the effect of T supplementation on neutrophilic response in the airway to wood smoke particle exposure as reflected in recovered sputum samples.  Volunteers who were found to be WSP responsive (>10% increase in the differential count of neutrophils in sputum) were randomized using a four subject block randomization procedure developed by our statistician. The randomization was carried out by the UNC Investigational Drug Service pharmacists, and they were the only unblinded personnel involved with the study in randomization blocks of four.  As outlined below in Gamma Tocopherol Dosing, we required two changes in the dosing procedure over the course of this study.  However, in data analysis, we treated each dose of gamma tocopherol equally and employed an intent-to-treat approach to our analysis. This protocol was listed in ClinicalTrials.gov and approved by the UNC IRB. All volunteers provided informed consent prior to participation in the study (NCT03444298, UNC IRB 17-2303).
L80: Volunteer Recruitment:
L81: All subjects were nonsmokers who were either healthy volunteers or volunteers with mild asthma with no acute respiratory illness who were responsive to a screening wood smoke challenge as previously described. Healthy volunteers had a FEV1 of >80% of predicted, and volunteers with asthma were required to have a FEV1 of ≥75% predicted without bronchodilator use. Asthma was defined by a history of episodic wheezing, chest tightness, or shortness of breath consistent with asthma, or physician diagnosed asthma and confirmed with a post-bronchodilator increase in FEV1 of at least 12%, or a clinical history of asthma after the age of 6.
L82: If persons with asthma used controller therapy (e.g. inhaled corticosteroids), they had to withhold this therapy for 2 weeks prior to WSP exposure. Persons requiring oral corticosteroid or biologic controller therapy were excluded. All asthmatics were also required to have either a positive skin test for aeroallergens present in North Carolina or a history of seasonal rhinitis symptoms.
L83: Volunteers were excluded if they had abnormal physical findings at the baseline visit, including but not limited to abnormalities on auscultation, temperature of 37.8° C, Systolic BP >150 mm Hg or <85 mm Hg; or Diastolic BP >90 mm Hg or <50 mm Hg, or pulse oximetry saturation reading less than 94%. These eligibility requirements are the same as those described in our initial screening protocol reports(1, 2) . This study was IRB approved and informed consent was obtained from all volunteers prior to initiating study.
L84: Woodsmoke Exposure Protocol:
L85: Details of the WSP exposure protocol have been previously reported (1, 2, 3) . The WSP chamber exposure used wood smoke generated by heating red oak wood on an electric heating element. Subjects were exposed to 500 μg/m3 WSP over a 2-hour period with alternating 15-minute periods of rest and exercise on a cycle ergometer at a level sufficient to produce a minute ventilation of 20 L/min/m2 body surface area. 500 μg/m3 is a level of WSP encountered in proximity to wildland fires. The mean characteristics of the controlled WSP exposure used in this study across 22 exposures for 11 volunteers were as follows: Fine PM mass concentration (µg/m3): 494.8+24.4, Coarse PM mass (µg/m3): 1.99+2.23, particle number (#/cc): 35532+12381, O3 (ppb) undetected, NO (ppb): 0.01+0.00, NO2 (ppb) undetected, total hydrocarbon (ppm) 4.13+0.50, SO2 (ppb) undetected, temperature (ºC): 22.01+0.01 and relative humidity (%RH): 40.01+0.05.  Endotoxin was not assessed in these samples, but in a prior study (3) endotoxin was not detected in recovered PM samples.
L86: Gamma Tocopherol Dosing:
L87: Gamma tocopherol was administered in custom made softgels provided by Eisai Food & Chemical Co., Ltd. Tokyo, Japan containing 700 mg of tocopherols, 89.5% of which is d-gamma tocopherol (one dose of two geltabs contains 1,253 mg of gamma tocopherol). Safflower oil was used as a placebo. The original dosing scheme was 2 geltabs every 12 hours for 4 doses. However, after 4 volunteers were treated using this regimen, a companion study using this regimen for ozone challenge revealed that it had no effect on O3-induced airway neutrophilia. Thus, a decision was made to modify the dosing schedule to a seven-day daily dose regimen which had been effective in inhibiting endotoxin-induced inflammation. Four volunteers received this dose. During an 18-month protocol pause necessitated by the COVID-19 pandemic, the original stock of gamma tocopherol expired and due to supply chain issues could not be reordered from the original supplier. Thus, commercially available Gamma E Gems, manufactured by Carlson Labs was used to complete the study. Each capsule contained 577mg of gamma tocopherol, as documented in a Certificate of Analysis. Subjects took 2 capsules at each dosing, for a total dose of 1154mg, using a neutral oil capsule for placebo.
L88: Sputum and Blood Analyses:
L89: Induced sputum was collected and processed according to previously published methods (4, 5). Plasma concentrations for gamma tocopherol and its metabolites was assayed as previously described
L90: Brachial artery ultrasound:
L91: As described previously (6, 7), flow mediated dilation, a measure of vascular endothelial function, was measured by brachial artery ultrasound.  Images were acquired using a Philips EPIQ 7 ultrasound machine equipped with a high frequency (L12-3) broadband linear-array transducer. Baseline images of the right brachial artery were captured at end diastole. The flow mediated dilation was assessed after reactive hyperemia induced by inflating a pneumatic tourniquet applied distal to the antecubital fossa to a suprasystolic pressure for 5 min. Hyperemic images were captured for 90 sec following cuff deflation. Brachial arterial diameter was measured at baseline and at maximum dilation with customized software that utilizes edge-detection technology (Vascular Research Tools, version 5.9.0; Medical Imaging Applications).  Flow mediated dilation was calculated as the percent change in diameter induced by hyperemia.
L92: Echocardiographic measures of left ventricular mechanics and diastolic function:
L93: Left ventricular mechanics were assessed by measuring left ventricular strain using speckle tracking imaging.  Apical 4-chamber, 2-chamber, and long axis 2-dimensional ultrasound images of the heart were acquired and stored for subsequent off-line analysis.  Global longitudinal strain was quantified using advanced quantification software (TOMTEC Imaging Systems).  Similarly, pulsed wave Doppler transmitral flow velocity profiles and tissue Doppler profiles of the septal and lateral mitral annular motion were acquired and subsequently quantified.  Measures of LV diastolic function included the average of the septal and lateral peak early diastolic mitral annulus velocities (e’) and the ratio of the early (E) transmitral Doppler flow velocity to e’ (E/e’ ratio).
L94: Statistical Analyses:
L95: The study was analyzed in an intent to treat fashion, treating each gamma tocopherol dose equally. To analyze our primary endpoints (change in %PMNs from baseline in %PMNs at 6 and 24 hours after initiation of WSP challenge), we employed the Grizzle model (8), imputing for missing data using the methods of Little and Rubin (9). To assess the effects of treatment and longitudinal trajectories of secondary and exploratory endpoints, we used square root transformation to normalize data in which some values were zero, followed by analyses using the mixed model as implemented in GraphPad Prism 8.0 we employed in our screening protocol (1). This mixed model uses a compound symmetry covariance matrix and is fit using Restricted Maximum Likelihood (REML).
